@page "/docs/get-started/existing-app"

@* Adding IzzyBlazor to an app that already has pages, a layout and its own CSS. The Bootstrap
   layering was checked in Chromium against Bootstrap 5.3 and the .NET 10 template's app.css; the
   EditForm case against a .NET 10 app. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="existing-app">
    <Lead>
        IzzyBlazor can join an app that already has pages, a layout and its own styles. Set it up once, then use it page by
        page: your existing pages, forms and components keep working beside it.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Set it up</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor needs a Blazor Web App (the project shape with an <Code>App.razor</Code>) on .NET 10.
            Follow the <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/blazor-web-app">Blazor Web App</Anchor> steps, and the
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/webassembly-and-auto">WebAssembly or Auto</Anchor> ones too if you have a
            <Code>.Client</Code> project. The rest of this page is about what's already there.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Keep Bootstrap</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's CSS sits in cascade layers, so any CSS of yours outside a layer wins over it. That's what makes it easy
            to adjust, but it means Bootstrap, and rules like the template's <Code>a { color: … }</Code>, override IzzyBlazor's
            components: button links turn blue on blue and headings change size.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Put Bootstrap in a layer of its own, declared before IzzyBlazor's. Remove its <Code>&lt;link&gt;</Code> and import
            it at the top of your stylesheet instead, move rules that style bare elements (<Code>body</Code>, <Code>a</Code>,
            <Code>h1</Code>) into the same layer, and link your stylesheet before IzzyBlazor's. The order matters here because
            yours now declares a layer:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="css" Title="wwwroot/app.css">@AppCss</Code>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/App.razor" HighlightLines="1-2">@AppHead</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The first layer declared loses to the ones after it, so IzzyBlazor's components win over Bootstrap, while
            Bootstrap's classes keep styling your existing markup. Your rules outside a layer still win over both.
        </TextBlock>
        <Alert Status="Status.Info" Title="Your pages take IzzyBlazor's page defaults">
            IzzyBlazor sets the page's font, text color and background, and its focus ring. Once Bootstrap is in a layer, those
            apply to your existing pages too. To keep your own, set them outside a layer, in <Code>app.css</Code>.
        </Alert>

        <Heading Level="2">Name clashes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's components have plain names, such as <Code>Button</Code>, <Code>Card</Code> and <Code>NavMenu</Code>.
            When a component of yours has the same name, the build fails with
            <Code>RZ9985: Multiple components use the tag 'NavMenu'</Code>. The full template's own <Code>NavMenu</Code> is the
            usual one. Rename yours, or write its full name where you use it:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">&lt;MyApp.Components.Layout.NavMenu /&gt;</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A type with the same name, such as your own <Code>Status</Code> enum, gives an ambiguous reference instead. Qualify one
            (<Code>IzzyBlazor.Status.Success</Code>) or add an alias to <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@@using Status = IzzyBlazor.Status</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">One folder at a time</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>_Imports.razor</Code> applies to its folder and the folders under it. To bring IzzyBlazor in gradually, put
            <Code>@@using IzzyBlazor</Code> in a folder's own <Code>_Imports.razor</Code> instead of the one in
            <Code>Components</Code>, and only the pages there see its components. <Code>App.razor</Code> and any layout built
            with IzzyBlazor need it too, at the top of the file: without it, <Code>&lt;IzzyTheme /&gt;</Code> is read as an
            unknown HTML element, the build only warns, and the theme never renders.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="text">@Folders</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A page can use a new layout while the rest keep yours: build one with
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor> and set it with <Code>@@layout</Code>, or as the default
            layout of a folder in its <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Forms you already have</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's fields are Blazor input components, so they work inside your existing <Code>EditForm</Code>, beside
            Blazor's own inputs, with the validator you have:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@EditFormCode</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            They show their own label, hint and errors. Switch the <Code>EditForm</Code> to IzzyBlazor's
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor> when you want its layout and columns; it takes the
            same <Code>Model</Code>, <Code>FormName</Code> and submit events, and adds the DataAnnotations validator itself.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: every component, with what it does in each mode.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/izzy-theme">IzzyTheme</Anchor>: match your brand color and fonts.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/style-props">Style props</Anchor>: spacing, sizes and colors on any component.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string AppCss = """
        /* First: puts Bootstrap in a layer that IzzyBlazor's layers come after. */
        @import url("lib/bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css") layer(bootstrap);

        /* Rules for bare elements, in the same layer, so they don't reach IzzyBlazor's components. */
        @layer bootstrap {
            a, .btn-link { color: #006bb7; }
        }

        /* Everything else as before: outside a layer, these still win. */
        .content { padding-top: 1.1rem; }
        """;

    private const string AppHead = """
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/IzzyBlazor.Core/izzyblazor.css"]" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["MyApp.styles.css"]" />
        <IzzyTheme />
        """;

    private const string Folders = """
        Components/
          _Imports.razor           your usings, as before
          App.razor                @using IzzyBlazor at the top, for IzzyTheme
          Pages/
            Orders.razor           untouched
            Settings/
              _Imports.razor       @using IzzyBlazor
              Profile.razor        uses IzzyBlazor
        """;

    private const string EditFormCode = """
        <EditForm Model="Model" FormName="profile" OnValidSubmit="Save">
            <DataAnnotationsValidator />
            <TextField @bind-Value="Model!.Email" />
            <InputText @bind-Value="Model!.Nickname" class="form-control" />
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Save</button>
        </EditForm>
        """;
}
